  design_campaign were plan_unrealizable_as_designed, plan_exceeds_node,
  evidence_contradicts_design, design_contradicted, the two gate-2 repair edges
  and revised_approach; a user decision fit none, so the lead re-opened the design
  boundary by hand and reconciled it in prose (LEAD-LOG 1801, DECISIONS 1066).
  Why general: a correctness criterion is often shown wrong only by the first
  measurement of a served candidate (bit-exactness across accelerators is the
  field case), and P9 says the user's word is the only thing that moves a frozen
  value, so the graph needs that word's door in every run. Why an outcome and
  edge, not prose: revision rule 2 (prose that handles an undeclared route is an
  invented edge) and pave-spec 13, Invisible edge. Why not overload
  plan_unrealizable_as_designed: its meaning is a repo contradiction, and one
  outcome carrying two causes gives the design brief two readings (pave-spec 13,
  Ambiguous routing). Why no new check on the edge: the exit gate's check
  design_approved_by_user already requires the user's verbatim word, and a second
  check on the same claim before the same harm is the gate pave-spec 4.11
  excludes. Cost per run: zero unless it fires; the lead reads a record it wrote,
  and the review node's own re-entry rule decides the reviewer's read scope (a
  whole read on a new design entry) - this revision adds no scope clause. P9
  kept: the frozen text is untouched and the new criterion is a new registration.
  The door needed one more clause to work: comparators_preregistered asked for a
  registration timestamp "preceding every measurement artifact", which is false
  for any campaign that re-registers after its first measurement - the live run's
  committed_at is 2026-09-17T13:20:39Z against measurement artifacts from
  2026-09-16 - so the declared route would have sent its own success back to
  design or to a hand ruling at the next candidate_serving (the round-1 HIGH). The
  check now asks for a timestamp preceding every measurement artifact adjudicated
  against it: the property it protects (no registration written after the
  artifacts it judges) is unchanged, and the predicate is true for exactly the
  registrations that satisfy it.

